(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["cam-nutrition-exam-2-herbal-medicines-medical-marijuana-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana — Set 2 — PA Quizzes","category":"CAM / Nutrition","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"What general dosing principle does the lecture give for medical marijuana?","choices":["Load with a high dose, then reduce once symptoms settle","Follow the principle of low and slow","Dose by body weight, as with paediatric herbal dosing","Give a fixed 60 mg daily dose regardless of route"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states the general principle is low and slow, and notes that over 10 mg of THC at one time can produce euphoria. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"What is the legal nature of a medical marijuana authorisation in Florida, as described in the lecture?","choices":["A recommendation from a physician, not a prescription, for home use only","A prescription that may be filled at any retail pharmacy","A federal waiver issued by the Drug Enforcement Administration","An over-the-counter authorisation requiring no clinician involvement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes it as a recommendation from a physician rather than a prescription, allowed for home use only, approved by the state while still illegal federally. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which physiological processes does the lecture say the endocannabinoid system modulates?","choices":["Only pain and appetite","Pain, seizure threshold, appetite, digestion, and mood, with a possible role in intraocular pressure","Only immune function and inflammation","Thermoregulation and blood pressure exclusively"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists modulation of pain, seizure threshold, appetite, digestion, mood and others, and says it may play a role in intraocular pressure. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Complaints of false or misleading supplement advertising are handled through which agency?","choices":["The Federal Trade Commission","The Food and Drug Administration","The Drug Enforcement Administration","The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture routes complaints of false or misleading advertising, and adverse events, through the FTC. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which interaction does the lecture attribute to garlic that is relevant to HIV treatment?","choices":["It raises levels of antiretroviral drugs to toxic concentrations","It interferes with the effectiveness of indinavir","It interferes with the effectiveness of saquinavir","It has no documented interaction with antiretroviral agents"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states garlic has been found to interfere with the effectiveness of saquinavir, a drug used to treat HIV infection, and that garlic can thin the blood similarly to aspirin. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which explanations does the lecture offer for why hot showers relieve cannabinoid hyperemesis syndrome?","choices":["Possible disruption of hypothalamic thermoregulation, heat countering CB1 hyperstimulation, or cutaneous steal syndrome redistributing blood away from the gut","Direct antiemetic action of heat on the vomiting centre","Increased hepatic blood flow that speeds cannabis clearance","Reduced absorption of cannabinoids from subcutaneous fat"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture offers all three of these explanations, including CB1 receptors in the splanchnic circulation slowing gastric emptying. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Which factors does the lecture say affect the potency of an herbal product?","choices":["Only the plant species used","Climate, soil conditions, where it was grown, harvested and stored, and batch differences","Only the extraction solvent used in manufacturing","Only the dose printed on the label"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists climate, soil conditions, location grown, harvested and stored, and batch differences as determinants of potency. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"By what mechanism does St. John's Wort reduce the effectiveness of many other drugs?","choices":["It inhibits CYP3A4, raising the levels of co-administered drugs","It binds drugs in the gut and prevents their absorption","It displaces drugs from plasma protein binding sites","It induces CYP3A4, increasing the metabolism of many drugs"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture identifies St. John's Wort as a CYP3A4 inducer that increases metabolism of many drugs, including antidepressants and birth control pills. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"How does the lecture define a botanical?","choices":["An individual with supervised practical training in treating patients with herbs","The study and practice of using plant material for food, medicine, and health promotion","A term used primarily in Europe for treating and preventing disease with herbal medicines","A substance made from part of a plant, including the bark, roots, or leaves"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. This is the lecture's definition of a botanical. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Under the 2023 Florida card limits, what is the aggregate daily THC cap for non-smoking routes?","choices":["150 mg per day","350 mg per day","200 mg per day","4,200 mg per day"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states the aggregate THC total cannot exceed 350 mg per day, or 24,500 mg per 70-day supply, for non-smoking routes. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which conditions does the lecture list as reasons a patient should not use echinacea?","choices":["Diabetes, hypoglycemia, or use of blood sugar–altering drugs","Liver disorder or symptoms such as dark urine and jaundice","Autoimmune disorder, anxiety or depression, asthma, cancer, migraines, or seizures","Shellfish allergy, sulfa allergy, or pineapple allergy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists exactly these conditions as reasons not to use echinacea, while noting its interaction risk with medications is low. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What happened to rimonabant, and why is it relevant?","choices":["It was approved in the United States as an antiemetic and later restricted","It was a CB1 blocker approved in Europe in 2006 as an anti-obesity drug and withdrawn in 2009 for depression, anxiety and suicidal ideation","It was a CB2 agonist withdrawn because of hepatotoxicity","It remains in use in Europe as a first-line weight loss agent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives exactly this history and notes it was never approved in the United States. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which statement about FDA authority over dietary supplements does the lecture make?","choices":["The FDA must approve each supplement's purity before it can be sold","The FDA sets mandatory adverse-effect reporting for all supplements","The FDA reviews all supplement advertising before it may be published","The FDA was granted authority to establish good manufacturing practice regulations, which are not rigorously adhered to"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. DSHEA granted the FDA authority to establish GMP regulations, and the lecture notes these are not rigorously adhered to. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"How does the lecture characterise delta-9 tetrahydrocannabinol at the cannabinoid receptors?","choices":["A full agonist at CB2 with no CB1 activity","A competitive antagonist at CB1, which explains its psychoactivity","A partial agonist at both CB1 and CB2, with higher affinity for CB1","An inverse agonist at CB2 that reduces inflammation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states delta-9 THC is a partial agonist of both CB1 and CB2 receptors but has higher affinity for CB1. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"How do onset and duration compare between oral and inhaled medical marijuana?","choices":["Oral onset 60 to 90 seconds lasting 2 hours; inhaled onset 10 to 30 minutes lasting 6 to 8 hours","Oral onset 10 to 30 minutes lasting 6 to 8 hours; inhaled onset 60 to 90 seconds lasting 2 hours","Both routes have an onset of about 10 to 30 minutes and last 6 to 8 hours","Oral onset 2 hours lasting 60 to 90 minutes; inhaled onset 30 seconds lasting 10 hours"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives oral onset as 10 to 30 minutes with effect lasting 6 to 8 hours, and inhalation onset as 60 to 90 seconds lasting 2 hours. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which dietary supplements does the lecture identify as the most widely used of all?","choices":["Probiotic and enzyme preparations","Omega-3 fatty acid preparations","Multivitamins and minerals","Adaptogenic herbs such as ashwagandha"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states multivitamins and minerals are the most widely used dietary supplements, and that patients should take only one per day. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which herb does the lecture pair with a specific warning about use on the face of infants and young children?","choices":["Arnica","Lavender","Capsaicin","Peppermint"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture warns that peppermint oil should not be applied to the face of infants or young children because inhaling the menthol may cause serious side effects. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"From which plant source is the drug digitalis (digoxin) derived?","choices":["Willow bark","Cinchona tree","Periwinkle","Foxglove"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists digitalis (digoxin) as a derivative of foxglove. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which supplement is described as a precursor to glutathione and used to treat acetaminophen toxicity?","choices":["Alpha lipoic acid","Methylsulfonylmethane","N-acetylcysteine","Bromelain"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes N-acetylcysteine as a precursor to glutathione, an antioxidant and a mucolytic, used to treat or prevent acetaminophen overdose and toxicity. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which questions does the lecture suggest patients ask themselves about a supplement?","choices":["Whether it promises rapid improvement, seems too good to be true, rests on a single study or anecdotes, and who funded the research","Whether it is sold in a pharmacy and whether it carries a national brand","Whether it has been on the market more than five years and is widely advertised","Whether it is more expensive than similar products, implying higher purity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists exactly these questions for patients to ask themselves. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which mechanism does the lecture give for capsaicin's pain-relieving effect on the skin?","choices":["It blocks sodium channels in peripheral nerve endings","It reduces the amount of substance P, a chemical that carries pain messages to the brain","It stops the release of inflammatory cytokines and interleukins","It inhibits 5-alpha-reductase in the dermis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states capsaicin reduces substance P, which carries pain messages to the brain, and also has a thermogenic effect that increases local circulation. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which receptors make up the endocannabinoid system as described in the lecture?","choices":["Two G-protein coupled receptors, CB1 and CB2, along with other regulatory enzymes","Two ligand-gated ion channels, CB1 and CB2, plus transport proteins","A single G-protein coupled receptor with two binding sites","Two nuclear hormone receptors that alter gene transcription"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes the endocannabinoid system as two G-protein coupled receptors, CB1 and CB2, plus other regulatory enzymes. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which herb is described as a 5-alpha-reductase inhibitor that may help benign prostatic hyperplasia, though a National Institutes of Health report found no benefit?","choices":["Saw palmetto","Slippery elm","Arnica","Black cohosh"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes saw palmetto as a 5-alpha-reductase inhibitor that may help benign prostatic hyperplasia, notes a National Institutes of Health report found no benefit and the evidence is mixed, and mentions possible help with hair loss. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which are given as absolute contraindications to medical marijuana?","choices":["Severe cardiovascular, immune, liver or kidney disease","Chronic nonmalignant pain and terminal illness","Any concurrent use of central nervous system depressants","Acute psychosis, unstable psychiatric conditions, and hypersensitivity to cannabis, safflower oil or other components"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists these as absolute contraindications. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which take-home point does the lecture give about a clinician's stance toward herbal supplements?","choices":["Discourage all supplement use until a product is FDA approved","Recommend the cheapest available product to improve adherence","They should not be discounted merely because of personal bias or belief, since patients will use them anyway","Rely on a general web search to answer patient questions quickly"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The take-home points state supplements should not be discounted due to personal bias or beliefs, that patients are going to use these products, and that clinicians should facilitate communication. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which effects does the lecture attribute more to docosahexaenoic acid than to eicosapentaenoic acid?","choices":["Blood pressure lowering and improved cognitive function, plus fetal brain and eye development","Reduced inflammation and mild vessel dilation","Reduced platelet aggregation only","Lowering of low-density lipoprotein cholesterol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture attributes the mild blood pressure decrease and improved cognitive function more to docosahexaenoic acid, and fetal brain and eye development to it as well. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Why should patients taking multiminerals avoid salt substitutes?","choices":["To avoid an interaction with nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs","To avoid reducing the effect of blood pressure medications","To prevent the supplement from binding to milk or antacids","To prevent elevated potassium levels"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states that to prevent elevated potassium levels, patients taking multiminerals should not use salt substitutes. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which condition appears on the Florida qualifying list given in the lecture?","choices":["Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis","Type 2 diabetes mellitus","Benign prostatic hyperplasia","Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis is among the qualifying medical conditions listed per the Florida Department of Health Office of Medical Marijuana Use. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"How many days of supply may a certified Florida patient receive, per the 2023 update?","choices":["Two 90-day supplies per calendar year","One 30-day supply renewable monthly","Three 70-day supplies, or six 35-day supplies for smoking only","Unlimited supply once a card is issued"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states patients are certified for three 70-day supplies, or six 35-day supplies for smoking only. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which patients should avoid glucosamine, and why?","choices":["Patients with sulfa allergy, because glucosamine is a sulfur donor","Patients with shellfish allergy, because glucosamine is derived from proteins and shellfish","Patients with soy allergy, because glucosamine is extracted from soy protein","Patients with pineapple allergy, because glucosamine is a proteolytic enzyme"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture says glucosamine is derived from proteins and shellfish and to avoid it in those with shellfish allergies, reading labels carefully. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 27"}]};
